WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.770
- A debate is contentious.

00:00:01.770 --> 00:00:03.090
A debate is about winning.

00:00:03.090 --> 00:00:05.950
A conversation is about
truth seeking together.

00:00:05.950 --> 00:00:10.750
When I saw the "debate"
devolve into personal attacks

00:00:10.750 --> 00:00:13.220
I felt like that was a
loss for me as a viewer.

00:00:13.220 --> 00:00:16.610
Hey guys, I want to talk about
this new Netflix documentary

00:00:16.610 --> 00:00:18.210
called The Game Changers.

00:00:18.210 --> 00:00:19.620
It's all the buzz in town.

00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:21.150
My patients are asking about it.

00:00:21.150 --> 00:00:22.260
You're asking about it.

00:00:22.260 --> 00:00:24.950
So, I decided that I'm gonna
make a video on the topic,

00:00:24.950 --> 00:00:27.720
but instead of overloading
you with a ton of information,

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:30.810
I decided to source three of
the most popular questions

00:00:30.810 --> 00:00:33.400
that you have and three of
the most popular questions

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:34.540
that my patients have.

00:00:34.540 --> 00:00:36.450
And I'm gonna answer
them within this video.

00:00:36.450 --> 00:00:39.970
Full disclosure, not only did
I watch the entire documentary

00:00:39.970 --> 00:00:43.180
but I also watched both episodes
of the Joe Rogan podcast.

00:00:43.180 --> 00:00:44.950
The first where he had Chris Kresser on

00:00:44.950 --> 00:00:47.290
essentially debunking the documentary

00:00:47.290 --> 00:00:49.510
and then the second one where
he brought in James Wilks

00:00:49.510 --> 00:00:52.520
from the documentary to have
a debate with Chris Kresser

00:00:52.520 --> 00:00:53.510
about those qualms.

00:00:53.510 --> 00:00:55.770
That's in total about
seven hours of footage.

00:00:55.770 --> 00:00:56.690
I watched it all.

00:00:56.690 --> 00:00:58.210
I wanted to make sure I came prepared

00:01:01.436 --> 00:01:04.277
my baseline thoughts on the subject

00:01:04.277 --> 00:01:06.467
going into watching the documentary

00:01:06.467 --> 00:01:09.687
so that you could be aware
of my cognitive biases.

00:01:09.687 --> 00:01:11.277
Now, we all have a bias.

00:01:11.277 --> 00:01:14.047
In fact when I watched the
debate between James and Chris

00:01:14.047 --> 00:01:17.487
on Rogan's podcast, I saw
their cognitive biases showing.

00:01:17.487 --> 00:01:20.917
And I think if you're more
aware of my cognitive biases

00:01:20.917 --> 00:01:22.991
and where I'm starting,
my starting point is,

00:01:22.991 --> 00:01:25.657
you'll be able to make a better judgment

00:01:25.657 --> 00:01:27.387
as to whether or not the information

00:01:27.387 --> 00:01:30.347
I'm presenting you is accurate
or where it's coming from

00:01:30.347 --> 00:01:32.157
and why I think what I do think.

00:01:32.157 --> 00:01:34.447
As a board certified
family medicine doctor

00:01:34.447 --> 00:01:36.817
who's done a lot of
research on the subject,

00:01:36.817 --> 00:01:39.437
the recommendation I give
most often to my patients

00:01:39.437 --> 00:01:42.977
is a whole foods, primarily
plant focused diet.

00:01:42.977 --> 00:01:43.897
It doesn't mean vegan.

00:01:43.897 --> 00:01:45.667
It means getting the
majority of your calories

00:01:45.667 --> 00:01:47.237
from non-animal sources.

00:01:47.237 --> 00:01:49.867
But it also means it's
okay to eat fish, meats,

00:01:49.867 --> 00:01:52.127
albeit red meat in
moderation, and removing

00:01:52.127 --> 00:01:54.867
to the greatest extent
possible processed meats.

00:01:54.867 --> 00:01:57.397
Now, in this diet you'll
have a lot of fruits,

00:01:57.397 --> 00:02:01.357
vegetables, healthy fats like
avocado, nuts, olive oil,

00:02:01.357 --> 00:02:02.190
things like that.

00:02:02.190 --> 00:02:04.377
It's actually quite close
to the Mediterranean diet.

00:02:04.377 --> 00:02:07.037
We have to remember though
that nutrition advice

00:02:07.037 --> 00:02:09.317
should be really given
on an individual level,

00:02:09.317 --> 00:02:11.107
so I don't recommend
that to all my patients.

00:02:11.107 --> 00:02:12.747
That's sort of my general advice,

00:02:12.747 --> 00:02:16.157
and then we make little
changes based on that person's

00:02:16.157 --> 00:02:18.957
body, their health, their conditions

00:02:18.957 --> 00:02:20.517
and what they hope to achieve.

00:02:20.517 --> 00:02:22.897
So, while I've never
explicitly recommended

00:02:22.897 --> 00:02:25.467
the vegan diet to somebody,
if a patient comes in

00:02:25.467 --> 00:02:26.907
and wants to, I make sure they do it

00:02:26.907 --> 00:02:28.447
to the best of their abilities.

00:02:28.447 --> 00:02:30.987
Supplementing, blood
testing, and making sure

00:02:30.987 --> 00:02:33.027
they're planning to get
the right macronutrients.

00:02:33.027 --> 00:02:34.787
Let's get into my thoughts before we jump

00:02:34.787 --> 00:02:35.697
into the questions.

00:02:35.697 --> 00:02:37.777
I'm really happy this
documentary was made.

00:02:37.777 --> 00:02:40.977
It's debunking a lot of the
myths we've been propagating

00:02:40.977 --> 00:02:42.587
for such a long period of time,

00:02:42.587 --> 00:02:45.227
like we need to eat a
certain amount of meat

00:02:45.227 --> 00:02:46.717
in order to put on muscle.

00:02:46.717 --> 00:02:48.517
A lot of these have
been proven to be untrue

00:02:48.517 --> 00:02:49.897
and I'm happy that this documentary

00:02:49.897 --> 00:02:51.007
is putting it out there.

00:02:51.007 --> 00:02:55.057
In fact, I'm happy that
we're discussing nutrition.

00:02:55.057 --> 00:02:57.407
We're actually learning
about what constitutes

00:02:57.407 --> 00:03:00.797
good research, why we're
so mislead with the certain

00:03:00.797 --> 00:03:02.407
headlines that we see in media.

00:03:02.407 --> 00:03:03.997
I love that we're discussing this.

00:03:03.997 --> 00:03:07.807
I mean, this video, Rogan's
video, the documentary itself,

00:03:07.807 --> 00:03:10.697
all the response videos,
this is all healthy debate

00:03:10.697 --> 00:03:13.537
that we need to do more
and more often in nutrition

00:03:13.537 --> 00:03:15.067
so that we can raise the bar

00:03:15.067 --> 00:03:16.597
on the type of research we're doing.

00:03:16.597 --> 00:03:18.037
We can actually make a meaningful impact

00:03:18.037 --> 00:03:20.727
on the future generations
with nutrition research.

00:03:20.727 --> 00:03:22.587
That being said, the
science of the documentary

00:03:22.587 --> 00:03:23.667
is pretty solid.

00:03:23.667 --> 00:03:26.107
All the facts are well stated.

00:03:26.107 --> 00:03:28.207
There's sources for everything
that appear on screen.

00:03:28.207 --> 00:03:29.237
I love that.

00:03:29.237 --> 00:03:32.767
In fact in a lot of my
videos I try and put links

00:03:32.767 --> 00:03:34.657
for further reading
from major organizations

00:03:34.657 --> 00:03:38.140
like WHO, CDC, or if I'm
referencing a specific study

00:03:38.140 --> 00:03:40.087
I put that study in there as well.

00:03:40.087 --> 00:03:42.977
The one thing that I will
say is I do feel it was made

00:03:42.977 --> 00:03:45.577
from an activist scientist standpoint.

00:03:45.577 --> 00:03:47.624
The reason I have to make that a point is

00:03:47.624 --> 00:03:50.797
they didn't present any counter-arguments.

00:03:50.797 --> 00:03:52.297
And when you're making a documentary

00:03:52.297 --> 00:03:54.927
I think it's in the
documentary's best interest

00:03:54.927 --> 00:03:57.547
if the documentary's
interest is truth seeking

00:03:57.547 --> 00:04:00.107
to put all perspectives in
there, at least the most

00:04:00.107 --> 00:04:02.947
common ones so that we
can as viewers decide

00:04:02.947 --> 00:04:04.787
for ourselves what's real, what's not.

00:04:04.787 --> 00:04:06.237
Now, the experts that they had in the film

00:04:06.237 --> 00:04:07.527
are credible experts.

00:04:07.527 --> 00:04:09.467
They're some of the leading
experts in the world

00:04:09.467 --> 00:04:11.977
but when you only have
them speaking about it

00:04:11.977 --> 00:04:15.167
and no one else from the other
side of this conversation

00:04:15.167 --> 00:04:17.757
you're leaving a void in
there and it feels like

00:04:17.757 --> 00:04:20.847
it's been done intentionally
from an activist standpoint.

00:04:20.847 --> 00:04:23.217
Now, I don't want to put blame on anybody

00:04:23.217 --> 00:04:24.837
or assume why that's being done.

00:04:24.837 --> 00:04:27.007
I just think that it would have
been a more complete picture

00:04:27.007 --> 00:04:29.487
had we heard from the other
side of the conversation

00:04:29.487 --> 00:04:32.207
about perhaps how much meat is healthy.

00:04:32.207 --> 00:04:33.837
Is no meat healthy?

00:04:33.837 --> 00:04:37.057
And sort of get a little
bit more details on that.

00:04:37.057 --> 00:04:39.457
Now, James Wilks who is
the star and co-producer

00:04:39.457 --> 00:04:42.997
of the film explicitly stated
that his goal with the film

00:04:42.997 --> 00:04:44.897
wasn't to make everyone go vegan.

00:04:44.897 --> 00:04:46.937
It was to reduce the
amount of animal products

00:04:46.937 --> 00:04:47.770
that they're taking.

00:04:47.770 --> 00:04:48.603
I love that goal.

00:04:48.603 --> 00:04:50.277
I think we should all
be thinking about that,

00:04:50.277 --> 00:04:51.837
especially for the environment's sake.

00:04:51.837 --> 00:04:53.727
But what it feels like
in watching this film

00:04:53.727 --> 00:04:55.337
and speaking to many people about it,

00:04:55.337 --> 00:04:57.717
my patients included, the
vibe that everyone gets

00:04:57.717 --> 00:05:00.867
from watching it is that eating
any amount of animal product

00:05:00.867 --> 00:05:02.277
is incredibly unhealthy.

00:05:02.277 --> 00:05:04.747
And we're gonna be getting
into that as we move forward

00:05:04.747 --> 00:05:07.687
in this video, but I
feel that if your goal

00:05:07.687 --> 00:05:10.217
was to send one message but in reality

00:05:10.217 --> 00:05:11.977
or from a practical
standpoint you're sending

00:05:11.977 --> 00:05:13.927
a different message, I
think something needs

00:05:13.927 --> 00:05:14.760
to be adjusted.

00:05:14.760 --> 00:05:16.627
When you're comparing
animal food companies

00:05:16.627 --> 00:05:19.487
to tobacco companies
and we're talking about

00:05:19.487 --> 00:05:21.667
dangerous words like inflammation, cancer

00:05:21.667 --> 00:05:25.207
and referencing that only
in terms of animal products,

00:05:25.207 --> 00:05:27.937
you're essentially scaring
people off in totality from it,

00:05:27.937 --> 00:05:29.337
at least in their minds.

00:05:29.337 --> 00:05:31.747
So, we need to be really
careful from not only

00:05:31.747 --> 00:05:34.427
are we presenting all of
the information factually

00:05:34.427 --> 00:05:37.917
but also how is it being
understood by the audience.

00:05:37.917 --> 00:05:39.817
Let's start off with
the YouTube questions.

00:05:39.817 --> 00:05:42.784
The first one was the
most upvoted and it was,

00:05:42.784 --> 00:05:46.044
"Is it true that all
protein comes from plants

00:05:46.044 --> 00:05:48.390
"and that animals are
just a vehicle for that?"

00:05:48.390 --> 00:05:52.207
I think when we're making
these types of reductionist

00:05:52.207 --> 00:05:54.337
statements we can really get lost

00:05:54.337 --> 00:05:56.437
as to the practical value of them.

00:05:56.437 --> 00:05:57.369
Let's use a metaphor.

00:05:57.369 --> 00:06:01.607
All people were once
lower forms of organisms

00:06:01.607 --> 00:06:03.687
because of evolution, so
we were once bacteria.

00:06:03.687 --> 00:06:05.648
Does it mean we're technically bacteria?

00:06:05.648 --> 00:06:09.647
Not really, and the fact that
a cow can eat a ton of grass

00:06:09.647 --> 00:06:12.127
and make it into a lot
of protein does not mean

00:06:12.127 --> 00:06:13.597
that we can eat that
same grass and turn it

00:06:13.597 --> 00:06:14.430
into a lot of protein.

00:06:14.430 --> 00:06:16.627
There's differences
between animals and humans,

00:06:16.627 --> 00:06:19.007
so don't know what to
make of that statement.

00:06:19.007 --> 00:06:20.267
I hope that answers your question.

00:06:20.267 --> 00:06:22.334
Question number two, "Will going vegan

00:06:22.334 --> 00:06:23.507
"give me more energy?"

00:06:23.507 --> 00:06:26.013
First of all, it depends
what are you switching from

00:06:26.013 --> 00:06:28.397
and what are you switching to?

00:06:28.397 --> 00:06:30.127
If you're switching from a very unhealthy,

00:06:30.127 --> 00:06:32.497
let's say the American standard
diet where you're eating

00:06:32.497 --> 00:06:35.527
a ton of burgers and pepperoni
pizzas and things like that

00:06:35.527 --> 00:06:38.247
and then you switch to
a healthy vegan diet,

00:06:38.247 --> 00:06:39.867
you're gonna feel like
you have a lot of energy.

00:06:39.867 --> 00:06:42.607
But now on the other hand, let's
say you were eating already

00:06:42.607 --> 00:06:45.787
a Mediterranean diet and now
you switch to a vegan diet

00:06:45.787 --> 00:06:47.207
but one that's unhealthy.

00:06:47.207 --> 00:06:50.407
You're eating a ton of refined
carbs, processed foods.

00:06:50.407 --> 00:06:51.647
They're still vegan.

00:06:51.647 --> 00:06:53.817
You're not gonna see a
dramatic increase in energy.

00:06:53.817 --> 00:06:55.407
In fact your energy levels may fall.

00:06:55.407 --> 00:06:58.527
I don't think it's a magic
pill like in the documentary

00:06:58.527 --> 00:07:01.044
some of the athletes said,
"Oh, before I was performing

00:07:01.044 --> 00:07:04.057
"so horribly and now all of
a sudden I have more energy."

00:07:04.057 --> 00:07:05.747
If they were eating
healthy before and switched

00:07:05.747 --> 00:07:08.697
to a vegan diet, I don't
know how much we can say

00:07:08.697 --> 00:07:11.137
that it was the vegan diet
that caused it directly.

00:07:11.137 --> 00:07:13.887
Perhaps in individuals we can say that,

00:07:13.887 --> 00:07:15.417
but not as a general rule.

00:07:15.417 --> 00:07:17.077
We haven't seen the evidence for that.

00:07:17.077 --> 00:07:18.994
Question number three,
"How did you feel about

00:07:18.994 --> 00:07:21.297
"the experiments that
they showed in the film?"

00:07:21.297 --> 00:07:24.697
That was probably one of my
biggest issues with the film.

00:07:24.697 --> 00:07:26.957
I understand what they're going for.

00:07:26.957 --> 00:07:29.337
They're trying to debunk
a lot of these myths

00:07:29.337 --> 00:07:32.527
surrounding performance and masculinity

00:07:32.527 --> 00:07:34.867
and how you can be vegan and still do well

00:07:34.867 --> 00:07:37.137
in all these aspects of
your life and that's true.

00:07:37.137 --> 00:07:40.397
But I wish they didn't resort
to a lot of these examples

00:07:40.397 --> 00:07:43.177
that weren't scientifically meaningful.

00:07:43.177 --> 00:07:46.097
Like, what does it mean
to see triglycerides

00:07:46.097 --> 00:07:46.957
floating in your blood?

00:07:46.957 --> 00:07:48.956
What does it mean that
these three gentlemen

00:07:48.956 --> 00:07:51.287
didn't have the proper erections at night

00:07:51.287 --> 00:07:52.277
when they ate something else?

00:07:52.277 --> 00:07:54.207
It's not a controlled experiment.

00:07:54.207 --> 00:07:56.607
There is so many variables at play

00:07:56.607 --> 00:07:58.367
and the sample size is tiny.

00:07:58.367 --> 00:08:01.537
I understand why they had to
do these visual demonstrations

00:08:01.537 --> 00:08:03.927
because it works really
well in the documentary.

00:08:03.927 --> 00:08:06.497
In fact when producers
call me to do morning shows

00:08:06.497 --> 00:08:08.517
or daytime talk shows, they always ask me

00:08:08.517 --> 00:08:10.440
to bring in demonstrations just like that

00:08:10.440 --> 00:08:12.237
or little experiments.

00:08:12.237 --> 00:08:15.127
But unless I can say
they're scientifically valid

00:08:15.127 --> 00:08:17.207
or whatever it is I'm proving makes sense

00:08:17.207 --> 00:08:19.517
I don't feel that I should
do it because I don't want

00:08:19.517 --> 00:08:21.897
to mislead the audience by any means.

00:08:21.897 --> 00:08:24.037
All right, let's move on
to the patient questions.

00:08:24.037 --> 00:08:26.557
These are the ones that
are the primary questions

00:08:26.557 --> 00:08:28.017
that seemingly everyone had.

00:08:28.017 --> 00:08:30.967
The meat of the discussion if you will.

00:08:30.967 --> 00:08:33.434
Question number one, "I'm
looking to put on muscle

00:08:33.434 --> 00:08:34.534
"and I want to go vegan.

00:08:34.534 --> 00:08:36.137
"Can I still put on size?"

00:08:36.137 --> 00:08:38.427
Absolutely, yes, and I think the strength

00:08:38.427 --> 00:08:41.557
of this documentary was
debunking that old myth

00:08:41.557 --> 00:08:43.967
that you couldn't be
vegan and put on muscle.

00:08:43.967 --> 00:08:45.227
You absolutely can.

00:08:45.227 --> 00:08:47.437
You have to plan accordingly,
supplement accordingly,

00:08:47.437 --> 00:08:50.557
making sure that you meet all
of your amino acid demands.

00:08:50.557 --> 00:08:52.717
But outside of that, make sure you have

00:08:52.717 --> 00:08:53.957
a good workout routine.

00:08:53.957 --> 00:08:55.697
I'm actually gonna be showing
you my workout routine

00:08:55.697 --> 00:08:57.377
in a video coming soon.

00:08:57.377 --> 00:08:59.507
Are there any health
concerns with going vegan

00:08:59.507 --> 00:09:01.007
and can we all go vegan?

00:09:01.007 --> 00:09:02.997
The vegan diet is one that's appropriate

00:09:02.997 --> 00:09:04.147
for all life stages.

00:09:04.147 --> 00:09:05.517
They said it in the documentary

00:09:05.517 --> 00:09:07.137
and I couldn't agree more with that.

00:09:07.137 --> 00:09:09.387
It's not necessarily the
first diet that I reach for

00:09:09.387 --> 00:09:10.676
when I recommend it to my patients

00:09:10.676 --> 00:09:12.587
but it absolutely can work.

00:09:12.587 --> 00:09:14.787
It's just important
that it's appropriately

00:09:14.787 --> 00:09:16.227
and well planned.

00:09:16.227 --> 00:09:18.006
And I know in the Joe Rogan debate

00:09:18.006 --> 00:09:20.247
James made a point in saying all diets

00:09:20.247 --> 00:09:21.237
need to be well planned.

00:09:21.237 --> 00:09:24.557
This is true but the vegan
diet does require more effort

00:09:24.557 --> 00:09:26.377
in that you really need
to plan your protein,

00:09:26.377 --> 00:09:28.547
your supplementation, and you generally

00:09:28.547 --> 00:09:30.967
don't have to do that if you're
eating an omnivorous diet.

00:09:30.967 --> 00:09:34.507
If you do happen to go vegan
but do it in an unhealthy

00:09:34.507 --> 00:09:36.327
or an unplanned way, you could actually

00:09:36.327 --> 00:09:37.937
do serious harm to your body.

00:09:37.937 --> 00:09:41.377
You can develop B-12 deficiency
which will lead to anemia.

00:09:41.377 --> 00:09:44.127
You can develop other vitamin
or mineral deficiencies,

00:09:44.127 --> 00:09:46.347
most notably vitamin D and calcium.

00:09:46.347 --> 00:09:48.797
And also, if you're
trying to put on muscle,

00:09:48.797 --> 00:09:51.217
if you're not planning your
protein sources correctly,

00:09:51.217 --> 00:09:53.387
you could also get in trouble in that way

00:09:53.387 --> 00:09:55.637
but for more people that
shouldn't be much of a problem.

00:09:55.637 --> 00:09:59.107
And the mac daddy of them
all, the final question,

00:09:59.107 --> 00:10:00.997
the question that so many
people have asked me,

00:10:00.997 --> 00:10:03.017
the question I've even
pondered after watching

00:10:03.017 --> 00:10:04.417
the documentary myself.

00:10:04.417 --> 00:10:08.272
Are meat or animal products
as unhealthy as they say

00:10:08.272 --> 00:10:11.627
and do I need to get rid of
all of them to stay healthy?

00:10:11.627 --> 00:10:14.797
I rarely am so blunt in
answering questions like this

00:10:14.797 --> 00:10:16.257
because there's usually a lot of nuance,

00:10:16.257 --> 00:10:17.427
but the answer is no.

00:10:17.427 --> 00:10:20.223
I think the documentary
went one step too far

00:10:20.223 --> 00:10:23.697
in fear mongering people to eliminate meat

00:10:23.697 --> 00:10:25.037
out of their diets completely.

00:10:25.037 --> 00:10:27.306
In fact James on the
Joe Rogan podcast said

00:10:27.306 --> 00:10:30.417
that frankly that he believes
that no amount of meat

00:10:30.417 --> 00:10:31.767
is safe or healthy.

00:10:31.767 --> 00:10:32.937
I don't that's the case.

00:10:32.937 --> 00:10:35.247
I don't think evidence has
proven that to be the case.

00:10:35.247 --> 00:10:36.577
I think we just don't know enough.

00:10:36.577 --> 00:10:39.047
I think the research that
we have is really flawed.

00:10:39.047 --> 00:10:41.757
We have industry funded
research, underfunded research,

00:10:41.757 --> 00:10:43.937
research that's done with questionnaires.

00:10:43.937 --> 00:10:46.697
My answer would be if
you're eating a healthy,

00:10:46.697 --> 00:10:49.871
whole foods, plant focused
diet, and you eat some meat,

00:10:49.871 --> 00:10:51.667
I think that's a very healthy diet.

00:10:51.667 --> 00:10:54.917
I think eliminating meat
completely is a personal choice.

00:10:54.917 --> 00:10:56.407
I don't think it's one that you normally

00:10:56.407 --> 00:10:57.797
have to make for your health.

00:10:57.797 --> 00:10:59.867
Albeit nutrition is an individual thing

00:10:59.867 --> 00:11:00.934
and if your doctor recommends that

00:11:00.934 --> 00:11:03.507
there might be a different
conversation for you.

00:11:03.507 --> 00:11:06.357
But there's one thing that I
love about this documentary

00:11:06.357 --> 00:11:08.127
that I want all of us
to take away from it.

00:11:08.127 --> 00:11:09.467
We should all reduce the amount

00:11:09.467 --> 00:11:11.267
of animal products we're eating.

00:11:11.267 --> 00:11:12.967
We're overeating calories as is,

00:11:12.967 --> 00:11:16.137
specifically animal product,
processed animal product.

00:11:16.137 --> 00:11:18.167
We need to get this
garbage out of our diet.

00:11:18.167 --> 00:11:20.887
It's one of the main reasons
obesity is overflowing

00:11:20.887 --> 00:11:23.597
in the United States in
addition to refined carbs.

00:11:23.597 --> 00:11:27.607
So, yes, we should lower our
reliance on animal products.

00:11:27.607 --> 00:11:29.437
We should be eating more
fruits and vegetables.

00:11:29.437 --> 00:11:31.517
And in fact me doing Whole 30 right now,

00:11:31.517 --> 00:11:33.437
one of the takeaways that
I've gotten from this

00:11:33.437 --> 00:11:35.787
is that I should be eating
more fruits and vegetables

00:11:35.787 --> 00:11:37.547
and that I have been relying on getting

00:11:37.547 --> 00:11:39.937
all my protein specifically
from animal sources.

00:11:39.937 --> 00:11:41.904
So, this documentary did
bring awareness to that

00:11:41.904 --> 00:11:44.017
and I'm really proud of it for doing so.

00:11:44.017 --> 00:11:47.177
Just saying that we should
remove all animal products

00:11:47.177 --> 00:11:50.107
completely as James said,
not a huge fan of that.

00:11:50.107 --> 00:11:52.127
I don't think there's
evidence to back that up.

00:11:52.127 --> 00:11:53.590
I think when you compare a vegan diet

00:11:53.590 --> 00:11:56.667
to an American standard
diet, it does an amazing job

00:11:56.667 --> 00:11:58.537
of overturning illness even.

00:11:58.537 --> 00:12:00.737
But when you compare a
vegan diet to someone

00:12:00.737 --> 00:12:03.307
who's otherwise healthy and omnivorous,

00:12:03.307 --> 00:12:05.107
I've never really seen evidence to show

00:12:05.107 --> 00:12:06.437
that vegan diet is superior.

00:12:06.437 --> 00:12:07.842
And the interesting part about it is

00:12:07.842 --> 00:12:12.837
most if not all major
organizations agree with that.

00:12:12.837 --> 00:12:15.429
James made a really big deal
in the podcast saying how

00:12:15.429 --> 00:12:19.567
Chris Kresser's views on
nutrition were not matching

00:12:19.567 --> 00:12:21.127
scientific consensus.

00:12:21.127 --> 00:12:24.237
Well, James's views aren't
matching scientific consensus.

00:12:24.237 --> 00:12:26.794
I haven't seen any guidance
from the WHO or CDC

00:12:26.794 --> 00:12:29.077
to say that we should not be eating

00:12:29.077 --> 00:12:30.377
animal products all together.

00:12:30.377 --> 00:12:32.147
I've seen that they recommend
that we should increase

00:12:32.147 --> 00:12:35.577
our vegetable and fruit intake,
decrease our processed food,

00:12:35.577 --> 00:12:38.407
refined carbs, our red
meats, our processed meats,

00:12:38.407 --> 00:12:40.347
but to say to remove all animal products

00:12:40.347 --> 00:12:42.677
is not scientific consensus.

00:12:42.677 --> 00:12:44.667
I want to leave you with
some thoughts that I had

00:12:44.667 --> 00:12:46.971
about the Joe Rogan
podcast where James Wilks

00:12:46.971 --> 00:12:51.167
and Chris Kresser debated
about the documentary.

00:12:51.167 --> 00:12:53.277
I know Joe Rogan framed this as a debate

00:12:53.277 --> 00:12:55.887
where Chris debunked the documentary.

00:12:55.887 --> 00:12:59.281
James saw that episode and
was very upset about it

00:12:59.281 --> 00:13:02.067
and he wanted them to
sort of battle it out

00:13:02.067 --> 00:13:03.727
or duke it out with their information.

00:13:03.727 --> 00:13:06.247
I don't necessarily want to see a debate

00:13:06.247 --> 00:13:07.497
from these two gentlemen.

00:13:07.497 --> 00:13:09.017
I want to see a conversation.

00:13:09.017 --> 00:13:12.317
A debate is contentious,
a debate is about winning.

00:13:12.317 --> 00:13:15.227
A conversation is about
truth seeking together.

00:13:15.227 --> 00:13:20.017
When I saw the debate
devolve into personal attacks

00:13:20.017 --> 00:13:22.547
I felt like that was a
loss for me as a viewer.

00:13:22.547 --> 00:13:25.067
I think that these guys are so smart.

00:13:25.067 --> 00:13:27.967
They've done countless hours
of research between them

00:13:27.967 --> 00:13:30.177
and they can bring a
lot to the conversation.

00:13:30.177 --> 00:13:32.427
And I think both of these gentlemen agreed

00:13:32.427 --> 00:13:35.317
more than they disagreed and
it didn't come off that way

00:13:35.317 --> 00:13:37.137
by the way they were
talking with one another.

00:13:37.137 --> 00:13:39.137
It almost seemed like they
disagreed on everything.

00:13:39.137 --> 00:13:41.487
It's not true and I think
their only real point

00:13:41.487 --> 00:13:45.897
of contention is should you
go 100% animal product free

00:13:45.897 --> 00:13:48.577
or go mostly animal product free.

00:13:48.577 --> 00:13:50.657
And I think that's such
a minute difference

00:13:50.657 --> 00:13:53.743
that our research with the low
quality that it is right now,

00:13:53.743 --> 00:13:56.287
I can't really tell which
one of them is right.

00:13:56.287 --> 00:13:58.489
So, I still say to my
patients that they should

00:13:58.489 --> 00:14:00.817
eat a predominantly plant based diet

00:14:00.817 --> 00:14:02.867
with some animal products in there,

00:14:04.947 --> 00:14:06.417
I put together a playlist for you

00:14:06.417 --> 00:14:08.897
of all my nutrition videos
including going vegan

00:14:08.897 --> 00:14:10.497
for 30 days so click here.

00:14:10.497 --> 00:14:12.327
And if you have any residual questions,

00:14:12.327 --> 00:14:14.227
leave them down below
in the comments section

00:14:14.227 --> 00:14:15.727
because I want to answer them.

